Q: How to properly get remote IP address in a Blazor-server application? A: <p>This code worked for me when I insert it in host.cshtml file</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>string? ip = HttpContext.Connection?.RemoteIpAddress.ToString();
</code></pre>

Q: How to migrate from xml2js to fast-xml-parser without changing the parser output? A: <p>The previous answer is a step in the right direction and probably cover the cases in StackBlitz.<br />
In addition to that configuration I added a few extra configurations leading to an almost exact match in output:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const xmlParser = new XMLParser({
    ignoreAttributes: false,
    ignoreDeclaration: true,
    attributeNamePrefix: '',
    attributesGroupName: '$',
    textNodeName: '_',
    parseTagValue: false,
    parseAttributeValue: false,
    isArray: (name, jpath, isLeafNode, isAttribute) =&gt; {
        return !isAttribute &amp;&amp; jpath.indexOf('.') !== -1;
    },
});
</code></pre>
<p>The only difference between the parsers above is the whitespace that is trimmed on fast-xml-parser and not trimmed on xml2js. This can be mitigated with the config <code>trimValues: false</code>. However, xml2js does trim when the values are only white spaces (and line-breaks). Therefore, to make an exact match I used the following configuration:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const xmlParser = new XMLParser({
    ignoreAttributes: false,
    ignoreDeclaration: true,
    attributeNamePrefix: '',
    attributesGroupName: '$',
    textNodeName: '_',
    parseTagValue: false,
    parseAttributeValue: false,
    isArray: (name, jpath, isLeafNode, isAttribute) =&gt; {
        return !isAttribute &amp;&amp; jpath.indexOf('.') !== -1;
    },
    trimValues: false,
    tagValueProcessor: (_tagName, tagValue) =&gt; {
        if (typeof tagValue === 'string' &amp;&amp; tagValue.includes('\n') &amp;&amp; tagValue.trim().length === 0) {
            return '';
        }
        return tagValue;
    },
});
</code></pre>
<h2>Here is a breakdown of changes:</h2>
<ul>
<li>fast-xml-parser ignores attributes: <code>ignoreAttributes: false</code>.</li>
<li>xml2js ignores the xml declaration: <code>ignoreDeclaration: true</code>.</li>
<li>fast-xml-parser uses the following name prefix for attributes and it can be replaced by an empty string (as xml2js does): <code>attributeNamePrefix: ''</code>.</li>
<li>xml2j uses <code>'$'</code> for group names: <code>attributesGroupName: '$'</code>.</li>
<li>fast-xml-parser uses <code>'#text'</code> for text nodes and xml2j uses <code>'_'</code>: <code>textNodeName: '_'</code></li>
<li>xml2j doesn't parse tag or attribute values: <code>parseTagValue: false, parseAttributeValue: false</code>.</li>
<li>xml2j uses arrays for most of the document, besides the root tag, the following function achieves this in fast-xml-parser:</li>
</ul>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>isArray: (name, jpath, isLeafNode, isAttribute) =&gt; {
    return !isAttribute &amp;&amp; jpath.indexOf('.') !== -1;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This function is more dynamic than the one from the other answer because it works in every document regardless of the name of the root tag, also with multiple root tags. This works because nested tags will have <code>'.'</code> in the jpath (root.child).</p>
<p>Lastly, if not trimming values is important: <code>trimValues: false</code> in combination with the following processor will replicate xml2js. That is because xml2js doesn't trim values but it doesn't include empty values either:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>trimValues: false,
tagValueProcessor: (_tagName, tagValue) =&gt; {
    if (typeof tagValue === 'string' &amp;&amp; tagValue.includes('\n') &amp;&amp; tagValue.trim().length === 0) {
        return '';
    }
    return tagValue;
}
</code></pre>
<p>I'm not sure if this is an exhaustive replacement, but it work on all the test data I had available.</p>
